1.1.text:
Part One Prevention of Acts of Violence against Women and Children

Article 23

1. The responsibility of the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. organizing socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations working in the field of women's empowerment and child protection; and
g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.
2. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:

a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual;
g. informatics and communication; and
h. peace and order.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their respective fields of duty in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 5

Every woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory acts;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her abilities;
e. obtain work leave with wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the implementation of her work or profession against matters that may threaten her safety and/or health in relation to women's reproductive function; and
h. ownership and management of joint property in marriage in accordance with statutory regulations.

Article 31

1. Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:
a. promotive, improving so that health status increases;
b. preventive, preventing from contracting disease;
c. curative, curing a person from illness physically and psychologically;
d. rehabilitative, maintaining so that a person who has not fully recovered can return to being fit as before.
2. Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, can be accessed 24 (twenty-four) hours, is of quality and is carried out in accordance with health service standards.
3. Women and children victims of acts of violence have the right to health services free of charge, including:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services;
c. psychosocial counseling; and
d. Visum et Repertum.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by a Regent Regulation.

